{
  "dom_id": "step-q135300",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?",
  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/3775fdcb48fd015097b484b6d5ebaefcecd4ef10298cb18b34226a8eae1be1ef.png)",
